BC-250 (Cyan Skillfish, gfx1013): keep user SDMA queues off engine 0

On this board SDMA0 does not deliver its completion interrupt. At every boot,
before any workload runs, the driver logs:

    amdgpu 0000:01:00.0: Fence fallback timer expired on ring sdma0
    amdgpu 0000:01:00.0: Fence fallback timer expired on ring sdma0
    amdgpu 0000:01:00.0: ring sdma0 uses VM inv eng 12 on hub 0
    amdgpu 0000:01:00.0: ring sdma1 uses VM inv eng 13 on hub 0

That message comes from amdgpu_fence_fallback(), which warns only when
amdgpu_fence_process() found an already-completed fence:

    if (amdgpu_fence_process(ring))
            dev_warn(ring->adev->dev,
                     "Fence fallback timer expired on ring %s\n", ...);

So the work finished and the interrupt was lost -- the engine is not stuck.
SDMA1 comes up clean and never produces the warning.

Userspace pays for this twice:

  HSA_ENABLE_SDMA=1  a copy queued on SDMA0 completes but never signals, and
                     the caller spins. Measured: 199% CPU, state R, no progress
                     for minutes on a warmup that takes seconds otherwise.

  HSA_ENABLE_SDMA=0  the runtime falls back to blit kernels, whose completion
                     signalling is unreliable the same way. A batch of large
                     host-to-device copies is reported done early and exactly
                     one 2 MiB chunk of one of them never lands:

                       1048434 of 4014080 elements differ, first at index 0
                       1048471 of 5242880 elements differ, first at index 1048576
                       1048461 of 5242880 elements differ, first at index 1048576

                     2^20 fp16 elements is exactly 2 MiB, and the first bad
                     index is a multiple of it. Measured with no convolution
                     involved: upload a tensor, read it back, compare. With a
                     sync after each upload instead of a batch, 48/48 clean.

allocate_sdma_queue() takes the first free bit and derives the engine as

    sdma_engine_id = sdma_id % kfd_get_num_sdma_engines(dev)

so with two engines the even ids land on engine 0, and the first queue of every
process lands there. Clearing those bits leaves engine 0 to the kernel's own
ring and hands user queues only to engine 1.

Costs half the SDMA queue slots. Off by default so it can be A/B tested against
the same build:  amdgpu.bc250_skip_sdma0=1

Community precedent: neoney's clr-prefer-sdma1 does the same steering inside
ROCclr ("The BC-250's SDMA0 H2D path stalls; prefer SDMA1 when it is
available"). Doing it in KFD covers every client and survives a ROCm rebuild.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/kfd_device_queue_manager.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/kfd_device_queue_manager.c
@@ -25,6 +25,7 @@
 #include <linux/ratelimit.h>
 #include <linux/printk.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
 #include <linux/list.h>
 #include <linux/types.h>
@@ -131,6 +132,12 @@
+static int bc250_skip_sdma0;
+module_param(bc250_skip_sdma0, int, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(bc250_skip_sdma0,
+	"BC-250: 0=off 1=keep user SDMA queues off engine 0 (its completion IRQ is lost)");
+#define BC250_PCI_DEVICE_ID 0x13FE
+
 static void init_sdma_bitmaps(struct device_queue_manager *dqm)
 {
 	bitmap_zero(dqm->sdma_bitmap, KFD_MAX_SDMA_QUEUES);
@@ -142,6 +149,21 @@
 	bitmap_clear(dqm->xgmi_sdma_bitmap, 0, kfd_get_num_xgmi_sdma_engines(dqm->dev) *
 			dqm->dev->kfd->device_info.num_reserved_sdma_queues_per_engine);
+
+	if (bc250_skip_sdma0 &&
+	    dqm->dev->adev->pdev->device == BC250_PCI_DEVICE_ID) {
+		unsigned int neng = kfd_get_num_sdma_engines(dqm->dev);
+		unsigned int nq = get_num_sdma_queues(dqm);
+		unsigned int bit;
+
+		if (neng > 1) {
+			for (bit = 0; bit < nq; bit++)
+				if (bit % neng == 0)
+					clear_bit(bit, dqm->sdma_bitmap);
+			dev_info_once(dqm->dev->adev->dev,
+				      "BC-250: user SDMA queues restricted to engines 1..%u\n",
+				      neng - 1);
+		}
+	}
 }
